<p>The amendment also hardened fines — Section 6 now requires a fine that is just and reasonable, paid to the victim — and, through the aggravated categories in Sections 5 and 9, extended the harshest ranges to offences against children below specified ages and by persons in positions of trust.</p>

<h2>Why the trap recurs</h2>
<p>The error is structural, not careless. By the time a Fast Track Special Court sentences, the amended text has been the only text counsel and court have read for years; bare acts in circulation print the current section, not its history. The charge, framed under &ldquo;Section 6 POCSO&rdquo;, looks identical under either version. Unless someone asks the Article 20(1) question — what did this section say on the offence date? — a sentence of 20 or 25 years in a pre-amendment case appears facially regular. The November 2026 Delhi High Court decision in <strong>Dilip Kumar @ Tinu</strong> illustrates both the trap and the repair: conviction affirmed on the child&rsquo;s testimony, but the 25-year term — fixed within the amended range for a February 2019 offence — reduced to 10 years, the unamended minimum, following Sonu Kushwaha. The reduction, the Court noted, does not amount to retrospective application of leniency; it is simply the application of the correct law.</p>

<h2>A short protocol for each side</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>For the defence:</strong> plead the offence-date version of every punishing section in the sentencing hearing and in appeal grounds; where the trial court has applied the amended minimum, the point is one of pure law, available even where the merits challenge is abandoned.</p>
  <p><strong>For the prosecution:</strong> verify the operative dates before pressing for enhanced minimums — an illegally severe sentence is a built-in appellate vulnerability that can cost finality in an otherwise sound conviction.</p>
  <p><strong>For both:</strong> where a course of conduct straddles 16 August 2019, the acts must be separated; only post-amendment acts can draw post-amendment punishment.</p>
</div>

<h2>The sentencing discretion that survives</h2>
<p>Identifying the applicable version fixes the range; it does not fix the sentence. Within the unamended Section 6 range of ten years to life, courts weigh the offender&rsquo;s age and antecedents against the victim&rsquo;s age, the repetition of the acts and accompanying threats — the same proportionality exercise as in any grave offence. The 2019 amendment removed much of that discretion prospectively; for the pre-amendment docket still working through the system, the discretion, and the responsibility that comes with it, remain.</p>
<div class="note">This explainer states the statutory position for general information; sentencing in any particular case turns on its record. Offences from 1 July 2024 onward are read with the BNS and BNSS where general criminal law applies alongside POCSO.</div>
